WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:04.960
With just three words, Chris Hemsworth's Thor
spoke for us all in Avengers: Endgame.

00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:08.750
When Captain America picked up Thor's hammer
Mjolnir in the battle scene at the end of

00:00:08.750 --> 00:00:12.260
the movie, the God of Thunder looked up and
said what we were all thinking:

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:13.340
"I knew it."

00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:17.480
It was one of the most powerful scenes in
the whole film, but the exchange didn't always

00:00:17.480 --> 00:00:19.300
play out as fans saw it on screen.

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:24.580
In Slashfilm's extensive oral history of Avengers:
Endgame's final battle, the creative powers

00:00:24.580 --> 00:00:27.760
behind the film revealed the truth about Thor's
"I knew it!"

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:28.260
line.

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:33.260
Endgame editor Jeff Ford and co-writer Christopher
Markus explained that it actually took a bunch

00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:37.720
of takes with Hemsworth before directors Joe
and Anthony Russo found the line that struck

00:00:37.720 --> 00:00:38.420
the right tone.

00:00:38.680 --> 00:00:41.300
As it turns out, all of that happened during
reshoots.

00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:42.480
Ford told Slashfilm:

00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:46.600
"It was during a reshoot that we shot a bunch
of lines from Thor for what he would say,

00:00:46.610 --> 00:00:48.350
but the best one was, 'I knew it.

00:00:48.350 --> 00:00:52.740
We had [Hemsworth] on that rock and we were
like, 'Say this, and this, now this.'"

00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:57.050
Captain America's big moment with Mjolnir
was planned out early in the creative process

00:00:57.050 --> 00:00:59.930
for Avengers: Endgame, but Thor's reaction
wasn't.

00:00:59.930 --> 00:01:02.060
The filmmakers struggled to get it just right.

00:01:02.060 --> 00:01:06.720
Different emotions and different intonations
made for very different meanings, and the

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:10.790
Endgame team put Hemsworth through a pretty
intense day of filming to really nail down

00:01:10.790 --> 00:01:12.020
the perfect delivery.

00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:13.640
Markus explained to Slashfilm:

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:16.440
"They might have all been 'I knew it,' just
with different spins.

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:17.940
The one that's in the script is happy.

00:01:18.170 --> 00:01:22.540
I'm sure there was some jealousy, some resentment,
some shock it's such a happy moment for the

00:01:22.541 --> 00:01:26.420
audience that I'm glad we went with the happy
one for Thor, because he's sort of the voice

00:01:26.420 --> 00:01:28.220
of the audience going, 'F--- yes!'"

00:01:28.330 --> 00:01:32.940
According to Avengers: Endgame co-writer Stephen
McFeely, the Marvel crew knew as early as

00:01:32.950 --> 00:01:38.000
the summer of 2015 that they wanted to have
Captain America wield Mjolnir at some point

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:41.080
in the film, but they didn't have the big
moment planned out in advance.

00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:45.960
As fans recall, Captain America attempted
to pick up Mjolnir in 2015's Avengers: Age

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:48.570
of Ultron, but only shifted the hammer slightly.

00:01:48.570 --> 00:01:53.060
Like Thor's celebratory line in Endgame, Cap's
ties to Mjolnir changed over time.

00:01:53.240 --> 00:01:57.960
During an appearance on Josh Horowitz's Happy
Sad Confused podcast, Endgame co-director

00:01:57.970 --> 00:02:02.280
Anthony Russo explained that Marvel creatives
always understood that Captain America was

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:03.870
worthy of wielding Mjolnir.

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:08.759
In fact, the character himself realized that
during Avengers: Age of Ultron when he manages

00:02:08.759 --> 00:02:09.810
to budge the hammer.

00:02:09.810 --> 00:02:13.870
Being the good guy that he is, Cap actually
chose not to pick up Mjolnir, as he didn't

00:02:13.870 --> 00:02:15.000
want to hurt Thor's pride.

00:02:15.260 --> 00:02:16.280
As Russo tells it:

00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:20.180
"(It was) In our heads he was able to wield
it, and he didn't know that until that moment

00:02:20.180 --> 00:02:22.020
in Ultron when he tried to pick it up.

00:02:22.020 --> 00:02:26.500
But Cap's sense of character and his sense
of humility, sort of out of deference to Thor's

00:02:26.500 --> 00:02:30.520
ego, you know Cap in that moment realizing
he can move the hammer decides not to."

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:34.980
Some fans have floated another theory about
Cap and Mjolnir: the hero may have become

00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:39.050
worthy of wielding the hammer due to the events
of Captain America: Civil War.

00:02:39.050 --> 00:02:43.460
It also seems possible that the loss against
Thanos in Infinity War could have changed

00:02:43.460 --> 00:02:44.860
Cap, making him more worthy.

00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:49.450
Regardless of when Captain America became
worthy, Avengers: Endgame editor Ford said

00:02:49.450 --> 00:02:52.990
the moment when he picks up the hammer had
more impact because it happened late in the

00:02:52.990 --> 00:02:54.180
Marvel film franchise:

00:02:54.280 --> 00:02:58.140
"I think seeing that realized is one of the
pleasures of a long series like this, where

00:02:58.140 --> 00:03:02.020
you've really been with these characters for
a long time, so when that happens, it's not

00:03:02.020 --> 00:03:04.460
satisfying unless you buy it, and I buy it."

00:03:04.540 --> 00:03:08.160
Just like Thor, the audience was cheering
for Captain America in that moment.

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:12.220
Not only was he saving Thor from Thanos, he
was showing that he's always been a truly

00:03:12.220 --> 00:03:13.320
mythic superhero.

00:03:13.460 --> 00:03:14.460
Like Ford said:

00:03:14.600 --> 00:03:15.840
"Cap is that guy."

